  "answer": "Sometimes context matters, but the target market is not necessarily decisive.\n\nManual practice:\n• Some trade marks are regarded as scandalous regardless of the market. The Manual identifies marks likely to promote racial vilification, religious intolerance or personal abuse as common examples (TMM/Part30/2/2/11).\n• The scandalous element must be obvious and up front: a mere suggestion or vague possibility of offence is insufficient (TMM/Part30/2/2/4).\n• For Indigenous subject matter of significant cultural, spiritual or religious significance, the Manual expressly requires consideration of the proposed goods and services, sensitivity to Indigenous people, ownership and other mitigating or aggravating factors. Evidence that the mark or proposed use would not offend the relevant Indigenous community may support reconsideration of a rejection ground (TMM/Part30/2/2/13).\n\nLimit of the supplied material: these passages do not establish a general target-market test for all offensive marks. They support considering context in the Indigenous subject-matter assessment, while making clear that some marks are unacceptable irrespective of market. Section 42(a) itself is not supplied, so this answer describes Manual practice, not the statutory wording."
